-- Q1: easy / L2 — Interfund advance vs transfer
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Morningside records a $400,000 advance from its General Fund to a Capital Projects Fund to cover preliminary construction costs until bond proceeds arrive. Under GASB standards, how should the General Fund report this interfund advance at year-end?', '["As a transfer out reducing fund balance by $400,000", "As an expenditure in the General Fund", "As an ''advance to other funds'' receivable on the balance sheet", "As a deferred outflow of resources"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34, interfund advances are reported as receivables (''advance to other funds'') in the lending fund and payables in the borrowing fund because repayment is expected. This differs from transfers, which are permanent shifts of resources with no repayment obligation. Choice A is incorrect because transfers are one-way movements that do not require repayment, whereas advances create a receivable-payable relationship between funds.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Blended vs discretely presented component unit
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ridgehaven created the Ridgehaven Improvement Authority (RIA) to manage downtown redevelopment. The city council appoints all five RIA board members, approves its annual budget, and can remove board members at will. RIA issues bonds backed by tax-increment revenues that are obligations of the city. The finance director must determine how to present RIA in the city''s financial statements. Under GASB 14 (as amended by GASB 61), what presentation method should the city use and why?', '["Blended presentation, because the RIA board is substantively the same as the city council and RIA''s debt is expected to be repaid from city resources", "Discrete presentation, because RIA is a legally separate entity with its own governing board", "Blended presentation, because the city appoints RIA''s board members regardless of other factors", "Discrete presentation, because tax-increment revenues are generated by private development activity rather than by the city"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 14 (as amended by GASB 61), a component unit should be blended when the governing body is substantively the same as the primary government and there is a financial benefit or burden relationship. Here, the city council appoints and can remove all RIA board members, and the city backs RIA''s bonds, creating a financial burden. Choice B is incorrect because legal separation alone does not require discrete presentation when the substantively-the-same and financial benefit/burden criteria are met.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Major fund determination
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Bellwood has total assets of $80,000,000 across all governmental funds and $20,000,000 across all enterprise funds. The Parks Improvement Fund, a capital projects fund, has total assets of $9,200,000. Under GASB 34, does the Parks Improvement Fund meet the quantitative criteria for major fund reporting?', '["No, because its total assets are less than 10% of total governmental and enterprise fund assets combined", "Yes, because any capital projects fund with assets over $5,000,000 is automatically a major fund", "No, because only the general fund is required to be reported as a major fund", "Yes, because its total assets are at least 10% of total governmental fund assets and at least 5% of total governmental plus enterprise fund assets"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 76, a fund qualifies as major if any element (assets, liabilities, revenues, or expenditures) is at least 10% of the corresponding total for its fund category AND at least 5% of the corresponding combined governmental-and-enterprise total. The Parks fund''s $9.2M is 11.5% of governmental assets ($80M) and 9.2% of combined assets ($100M), satisfying both thresholds. Choice C is incorrect because while the general fund is always major, other funds meeting the quantitative criteria must also be reported as major.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — GASB 87 lease: fund vs government-wide
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Crestview enters a 7-year noncancellable lease for office equipment with annual payments of $36,000 beginning January 1, 2026. The present value of lease payments is $217,830. The finance director asks how this lease affects the governmental fund statements compared to the government-wide statements. Under GASB 87, what is the most likely difference in reporting?', '["The governmental fund statements report the full present value as a capital outlay expenditure, while the government-wide statements report a lease liability and intangible right-to-use asset", "The governmental fund statements report only the annual lease payment as an expenditure, while the government-wide statements report a lease liability, intangible right-to-use asset, and amortization expense", "Both statements report identical lease liabilities and right-to-use assets because GASB 87 requires uniform treatment", "The governmental fund statements report a long-term lease payable, while the government-wide statements report an operating lease expense"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 87, leases are recognized as intangible right-to-use assets and lease liabilities in government-wide statements, with amortization and interest expense reported annually. However, governmental fund statements use the current financial resources measurement focus and modified accrual basis, so only the current-year lease payment is reported as an expenditure. Choice A is incorrect because governmental funds do not report the full present value as a capital outlay; they recognize only the annual payment due in the current period.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — GASB 54 committed fund balance
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Edgewood''s city council passes a resolution on October 15, 2026, dedicating $1,800,000 of General Fund resources to a future technology infrastructure upgrade. No purchase orders have been issued, and the project has not started. The council has not placed any legal restrictions on the funds. Under GASB 54, how should the $1,800,000 be classified in the General Fund balance?', '["Committed fund balance, because the city council''s resolution is a formal action at the highest level of decision-making authority", "Restricted fund balance, because the council dedicated the resources for a specific purpose", "Unassigned fund balance, because no purchase orders have been issued", "Assigned fund balance, because the resources are intended for a specific purpose but not formally constrained"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 54, paragraph 10, committed fund balance represents resources constrained by formal action of the government''s highest level of decision-making authority (typically the governing body). A city council resolution qualifies as such formal action, and the commitment can only be removed by the same level of authority. Choice B is incorrect because restricted fund balance requires externally imposed constraints from creditors, grantors, or laws of other governments, not internal governing body actions.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — MD&A classification under GASB 34
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, management''s discussion and analysis (MD&A) is classified as which type of information in a government''s financial report?', '["A supplementary schedule presented after the notes to the financial statements", "A component of the basic financial statements alongside the notes", "Optional information included at the discretion of management", "Required supplementary information presented before the basic financial statements"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 8, management''s discussion and analysis is required supplementary information (RSI) that precedes the basic financial statements. MD&A provides an analytical overview of the government''s financial activities in a narrative format. Choice B is incorrect because MD&A, while required, is classified as RSI rather than a component of the basic financial statements, which include the government-wide statements, fund statements, and notes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Evaluate pension plan reporting under GASB 67/68
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Ridgefield participates in two pension plans: a single-employer defined benefit plan for police and fire employees and a cost-sharing multiple-employer plan administered by the state for all other employees. The single-employer plan has a total pension liability of $18,500,000 and plan fiduciary net position of $14,200,000. The state plan allocates a proportionate share of 0.42% to Ridgefield, with total net pension liability across all employers of $2,800,000,000. Under GASB 67 and 68, how should the town report pension obligations in the government-wide statements?', '["Report both plans'' total pension liabilities on the statement of net position and disclose the fiduciary net position in the notes", "Report only the single-employer plan on the statement of net position because the town does not control the cost-sharing plan", "Report the single-employer net pension liability of $4,300,000 and the proportionate share of the cost-sharing plan''s net pension liability of $11,760,000 on the statement of net position", "Report a combined net pension liability based on the total of both plans'' funded ratios applied to total covered payroll"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 68, paragraphs 28-29, a single-employer plan reports the net pension liability (total pension liability minus plan fiduciary net position: $18,500,000 minus $14,200,000 = $4,300,000). For cost-sharing plans, paragraph 48 requires reporting the employer''s proportionate share of the collective net pension liability ($2,800,000,000 x 0.42% = $11,760,000). Choice B is incorrect because GASB 68 requires cost-sharing plan participants to recognize their proportionate share of the net pension liability on the face of the financial statements.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — GASB 77 tax abatement reporting impact
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Harborview enters a tax abatement agreement with Greenleaf Manufacturing, reducing Greenleaf''s property taxes by $750,000 annually for 10 years to incentivize construction of a new facility. The city''s total property tax levy is $42,000,000. Under GASB 77, what is the most significant reporting impact?', '["The city must reduce its property tax revenue by $750,000 and disclose the abatement program in the notes to the financial statements", "The city is required to disclose the abatement program in the notes, including the gross dollar amount of taxes abated, but the revenue reduction may already be reflected in the tax levy", "The city must report the $750,000 as an expenditure rather than a revenue reduction", "The city must recognize a $7,500,000 long-term liability for the total abatement commitment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 77, paragraphs 5-7, governments must disclose tax abatement agreements in the notes to financial statements, including the gross dollar amount of taxes abated during the period. The standard focuses on disclosure rather than recognition because the reduced tax amount is typically already reflected in the levy or assessment. Choice A oversimplifies the reporting impact because the revenue reduction is often built into the tax levy rather than being a separate adjustment, making disclosure the primary new requirement under GASB 77.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Net investment in capital assets calculation
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Brookshire reports the following in its government-wide statements: capital assets of $125,000,000, accumulated depreciation of $48,000,000, capital-related bonds payable of $62,000,000, unspent bond proceeds held in the Capital Projects Fund of $8,500,000, and a deferred amount on refunding of $1,200,000. Under GASB 34, what is the net investment in capital assets component of net position?', '["$24,700,000", "$15,000,000", "$23,500,000", "$16,200,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, net investment in capital assets equals capital assets net of depreciation ($125M minus $48M = $77M) minus outstanding capital-related debt adjusted for unspent proceeds ($62M minus $8.5M = $53.5M) plus deferred outflows on refunding ($1.2M), totaling $24,700,000. Choice B ($15,000,000) is incorrect because it omits both the unspent bond proceeds add-back and the deferred amount on refunding, computing only $77M minus $62M.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — GASB 33 nonexchange transaction categories
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 33, nonexchange transactions are classified into four categories. Which of the following is one of the four categories?', '["Exchange-like transactions", "Capital contribution transactions", "Intergovernmental service transactions", "Government-mandated nonexchange transactions"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 33, paragraph 7, the four classes of nonexchange transactions are derived tax revenues, imposed nonexchange revenues, government-mandated nonexchange transactions, and voluntary nonexchange transactions. Choice A is incorrect because exchange-like transactions, while discussed in GASB 33 for comparison purposes, are not classified as nonexchange transactions — they are a separate category where values exchanged are not directly equal.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Proprietary fund cash flow classification
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Maplewood operates a municipal electric utility as an enterprise fund. During fiscal year 2026, the utility pays $1,500,000 in interest on bonds that financed the construction of a power distribution facility. Under GASB 34, which category of the proprietary fund statement of cash flows includes this interest payment?', '["Operating activities", "Noncapital financing activities", "Capital and related financing activities", "Investing activities"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 112, proprietary fund statements of cash flows classify interest payments on debt issued for capital asset acquisition under capital and related financing activities. Since municipal utility bonds typically finance capital infrastructure, the associated interest payments fall within this category. Choice A is incorrect because unlike FASB ASC 230, which permits interest paid to be classified as operating, GASB requires interest on capital debt to be classified as capital and related financing activities.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: hard / L3 — GASB 87 implementation effect on debt-to-asset ratio
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Greenfield currently has operating leases for vehicles and equipment totaling $4,200,000 in annual payments over 6 remaining years. The government-wide statement of net position shows total liabilities of $38,000,000 and total assets of $95,000,000 before implementing GASB 87. The present value of lease payments at a 3.5% discount rate is $22,380,000. How does GASB 87 implementation affect the town''s debt-to-asset ratio?', '["The ratio decreases because the right-to-use asset offsets the new lease liability", "The ratio increases because adding equal amounts to both numerator and denominator increases a ratio that is below 100%", "The ratio remains unchanged because lease assets and lease liabilities are equal at inception", "The ratio increases because the new liability exceeds the new asset due to first-year amortization"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 87, both a lease liability and a right-to-use asset of $22,380,000 are recognized at commencement. While the dollar amounts are equal, adding equal amounts to a ratio below 100% always increases it: ($38M + $22.38M) / ($95M + $22.38M) = 51.4%, compared to the original $38M / $95M = 40.0%. Choice C is incorrect because equal additions to numerator and denominator do not cancel out when the starting ratio is not 1:1 — the mathematical effect depends on the original ratio.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Program revenues vs general revenues
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ashford reports the following revenues in its government-wide statement of activities: property tax revenue of $28,000,000, a federal highway construction grant of $5,200,000, parking meter collections of $1,100,000, a state-shared sales tax allocation of $6,400,000, and investment earnings of $890,000. Under GASB 34, what amount represents total program revenues?', '["$41,590,000", "$35,290,000", "$13,190,000", "$6,300,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, paragraphs 48-52, program revenues include charges for services and grants directly associated with specific functions. The parking meter revenue ($1,100,000) is a charge for services and the federal highway grant ($5,200,000) is a capital grant, totaling $6,300,000. Property taxes, state-shared revenues, and investment earnings are general revenues. Choice C ($13,190,000) is incorrect because it adds the state-shared sales tax to program revenues, but unrestricted intergovernmental revenues are classified as general revenues under GASB 34.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Internal service fund elimination in government-wide
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Weston operates a Fleet Management internal service fund that charges operating departments $3,600,000 annually for vehicle maintenance. The fund reports total expenses of $3,400,000 and a $200,000 operating surplus. In preparing the government-wide financial statements, the finance director must eliminate internal service fund activity. What is the most likely effect on total governmental activities expenses?', '["Governmental activities expenses decrease by $200,000 because the interfund markup is eliminated", "Governmental activities expenses increase by $3,600,000 because the internal charges are reclassified", "Governmental activities expenses remain unchanged because internal service fund charges are already embedded in departmental costs", "Governmental activities expenses increase by $3,400,000 because the full cost is added to governmental activities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 62, internal service fund activities are typically consolidated into governmental activities in the government-wide statements. The $3,600,000 charged to departments is already in governmental activities expenses. However, the internal service fund''s $3,400,000 in actual costs replaces the $3,600,000 in charges, eliminating the $200,000 interfund markup. Choice C is incorrect because while the charges are embedded, the consolidation adjustment removes the $200,000 markup, reducing total expenses.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Deferred outflows/inflows on pension expense recognition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The County of Ridgemont''s actuary reports the following changes for its single-employer pension plan: actual investment returns were $8,200,000 compared to expected returns of $6,500,000, and changes in demographic assumptions increased the total pension liability by $900,000. Under GASB 68, how do the investment returns and assumption changes affect pension expense recognition?', '["Both the excess investment gain and the assumption change are recognized fully in the current year''s pension expense", "The $1,700,000 excess investment gain is deferred and amortized over 5 years; the $900,000 assumption change is recognized immediately in pension expense", "The $1,700,000 excess investment gain is deferred and amortized over a closed period; the $900,000 assumption change is also deferred and amortized over the average remaining service life of employees", "Both amounts are deferred indefinitely and only affect disclosures in required supplementary information"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 68, paragraphs 33-36, differences between projected and actual investment earnings ($8.2M minus $6.5M = $1.7M gain) are deferred and amortized over a closed 5-year period. Changes in actuarial assumptions ($900,000) are recognized as deferred outflows or inflows and amortized over the average remaining service life of plan members. Choice B is incorrect because assumption changes are not recognized immediately — GASB 68 requires systematic amortization of all experience and assumption differences.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Capital outlay reconciliation from fund to government-wide
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ferndale''s General Fund reports capital outlay expenditures of $6,400,000 for the fiscal year. Of this amount, $5,100,000 is for new capital assets and $1,300,000 is for maintenance that does not extend useful lives. Current-year depreciation on governmental capital assets totals $4,800,000. In the reconciliation from fund statements to government-wide statements, what is the net effect on the change in net position?', '["Increase of $1,600,000 ($6,400,000 expenditures removed less $4,800,000 depreciation added)", "Increase of $300,000 ($5,100,000 capitalized less $4,800,000 depreciation)", "Decrease of $4,800,000 (depreciation expense added with no offsetting adjustment)", "Increase of $5,100,000 (capital outlay reclassified from expenditure to asset)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, the reconciliation removes capital outlay that qualifies for capitalization ($5,100,000) from expenditures and adds it to capital assets. The $1,300,000 in maintenance remains an expense in both statements. Depreciation expense of $4,800,000 is then added to government-wide expenses. The net effect is $5,100,000 minus $4,800,000 = $300,000 increase. Choice A is incorrect because only the $5,100,000 that qualifies as a capital asset is removed from expenditures, not the full $6,400,000 of capital outlay.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — GASB 54 fund balance categories
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 54, what are the five categories of fund balance in governmental funds?', '["Nonspendable, restricted, committed, assigned, and unassigned", "Reserved, unreserved, designated, undesignated, and encumbered", "Restricted, unrestricted, committed, uncommitted, and available", "Invested in capital assets, restricted, committed, assigned, and unassigned"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 54, paragraph 5, fund balance is classified into five categories in order of constraint: nonspendable, restricted, committed, assigned, and unassigned. This replaced the previous reserved/unreserved/designated framework. Choice B is incorrect because the reserved, unreserved, and designated classifications were the pre-GASB 54 framework that was superseded to provide more meaningful and consistent fund balance reporting.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Proprietary fund types
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB standards, which two types of funds are classified as proprietary funds?', '["Special revenue funds and capital projects funds", "Agency funds and investment trust funds", "General fund and debt service funds", "Enterprise funds and internal service funds"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 67, proprietary funds consist of enterprise funds, which account for business-type activities provided to the public, and internal service funds, which account for services provided to other departments or governments on a cost-reimbursement basis. Choice A is incorrect because special revenue funds and capital projects funds are governmental fund types, not proprietary funds.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Evaluate conduit debt reporting under GASB 91
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Tri-County Development Authority issues $25,000,000 in revenue bonds on behalf of Greenwood Hospital, a private nonprofit entity. The bonds are secured solely by Greenwood Hospital''s revenues, and the authority has no obligation to repay the bonds if the hospital defaults. Under GASB 91, which reporting treatment is most appropriate for the authority?', '["Report the $25,000,000 as a long-term liability on the government-wide statement of net position", "Do not report a liability but disclose the outstanding conduit debt in the notes, including a description of the arrangement and the amount outstanding", "Report the bonds as a contingent liability at 50% of face value under GASB 62", "Report the bonds in a fiduciary fund because the authority acts as an intermediary"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 91, paragraph 8, a government issuer that has no obligation to repay conduit debt beyond acting as issuer should not recognize a liability. However, paragraph 11 requires note disclosure of the general description, aggregate outstanding amount, and the fact that the government has no commitment beyond its role as conduit issuer. Choice A is incorrect because GASB 91 explicitly states that conduit debt should not be recognized as a liability when the issuer has no repayment obligation beyond acting as intermediary.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Evaluate pollution remediation obligation under GASB 49
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Millbrook discovers contaminated soil at a former municipal landfill site. An environmental consulting firm estimates remediation costs between $3,200,000 and $5,800,000, with no amount within the range more likely than any other. The city has received a compliance order from the state environmental agency. Under GASB 49, what amount should the city recognize as a liability?', '["$5,800,000, because the maximum estimated amount should be accrued when remediation is required by law", "$4,500,000, because the midpoint of the range should be used when no single amount is more likely", "$3,200,000, because when no amount in a range is more likely, the minimum of the range is recognized", "No liability should be recognized until actual remediation costs are incurred and paid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 49, when the government cannot narrow a pollution remediation obligation to a single best estimate and no amount in the range is more likely than another, paragraph 19 requires recognition of the minimum amount ($3,200,000). The compliance order establishes a legally obligating event triggering recognition. Choice B is incorrect because GASB 49 does not permit the midpoint approach — it follows the minimum-of-the-range principle consistent with the general GASB guidance for estimated liabilities.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Modified approach vs depreciation for infrastructure
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The County of Eastwood maintains a road network with a net book value of $53,000,000 ($85,000,000 cost less $32,000,000 accumulated depreciation). Annual depreciation is $3,400,000. The county spends approximately $2,800,000 annually on road preservation to maintain its condition target. The county considers switching to the modified approach under GASB 34. What is the most likely effect on reported expenses in the government-wide financial statements?', '["Total expenses decrease by approximately $600,000 because preservation costs ($2,800,000) replace depreciation ($3,400,000)", "Total expenses increase by $2,800,000 because preservation costs are newly recognized as expenses in addition to depreciation", "Total expenses remain unchanged because the modified approach merely reclassifies depreciation as preservation cost", "Total expenses decrease by $3,400,000 because depreciation is fully eliminated and preservation costs are already recognized"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, paragraphs 23-25, the modified approach replaces depreciation with expense recognition of preservation costs needed to maintain the established condition level. Since annual preservation costs ($2,800,000) are less than depreciation ($3,400,000), the switch reduces reported expenses by approximately $600,000. Choice B is incorrect because the modified approach eliminates depreciation — preservation costs replace rather than supplement depreciation expense.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Basis of accounting for fiduciary funds
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB standards, fiduciary funds use which basis of accounting?', '["Modified accrual basis", "Cash basis", "Budgetary basis", "Accrual basis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, fiduciary funds use the economic resources measurement focus and the accrual basis of accounting, recognizing revenues when earned and expenses when incurred regardless of when cash is exchanged. Choice A is incorrect because modified accrual is used only by governmental funds (general, special revenue, capital projects, debt service, and permanent funds), not fiduciary funds.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Statement of activities reporting format
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, the government-wide statement of activities uses which reporting format?', '["A traditional income statement format that reports total revenues less total expenses", "A net (expense) revenue format that shows each function''s expenses offset by program revenues", "A cash-flow format that categorizes receipts and disbursements by activity", "A budgetary comparison format that shows actual results versus appropriations"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 38, the statement of activities presents expenses by function or program offset by directly associated program revenues (charges for services, operating grants, and capital grants), resulting in a net expense or net revenue for each function. General revenues are reported separately below. Choice A is incorrect because the statement of activities does not use a traditional income statement format — its distinctive net-expense structure highlights each function''s burden on general revenues.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — GASB 87 short-term lease exception
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'On October 1, 2026, the County of Briarwood enters a 10-month lease for temporary office space at $8,500 per month with no renewal option. Under GASB 87, how should the county account for this lease in its government-wide financial statements?', '["Recognize a right-to-use asset and lease liability at the present value of lease payments", "Capitalize the total lease cost of $85,000 as a prepaid asset at inception", "Recognize a lease expense of $8,500 each month as a short-term lease because the maximum possible term is 12 months or less", "Recognize the full $85,000 as a lease expenditure in the period the lease is signed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 87, paragraph 16, a short-term lease is defined as a lease with a maximum possible term of 12 months or fewer, including any options to extend. Since this 10-month lease has no renewal option, the maximum possible term is 10 months, qualifying it for the short-term exception. Payments are recognized as expenses in the periods to which they relate. Choice A is incorrect because GASB 87''s asset-and-liability recognition requirements apply only to leases exceeding the 12-month short-term threshold.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Evaluate fiduciary vs governmental fund reporting under GASB 84
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Oakmont administers a scholarship program funded by a $2,000,000 endowment donated by a local philanthropist. The city invests the principal and distributes earnings to qualifying students selected by a committee appointed by the donor. The city retains 5% of investment earnings for administrative costs. Under GASB 84, which reporting treatment is most appropriate?', '["A private-purpose trust fund, because the city holds resources in a trust arrangement where the principal and earnings benefit individuals rather than the government itself", "A custodial fund, because the city is acting as a pass-through agent for the donor''s resources", "A special revenue fund, because the 5% administrative fee retention gives the city a financial interest in the program", "A permanent fund, because the principal cannot be spent and only earnings are distributed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 84, paragraphs 11-13, fiduciary activities include trust arrangements where the government holds resources for the benefit of individuals or entities other than the government. The scholarship program benefits individual students (private purpose), and the trust arrangement with the invested principal meets the private-purpose trust fund criteria. Choice D is incorrect because permanent funds are governmental funds used when the principal must be preserved and earnings benefit the government or its citizenry, whereas this program benefits specific private individuals selected by the donor''s committee.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Governmental fund types
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB standards, which of the following is one of the five governmental fund types?', '["Internal service fund", "Investment trust fund", "Permanent fund", "Enterprise fund"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34, the five governmental fund types are the general fund, special revenue funds, capital projects funds, debt service funds, and permanent funds. Permanent funds account for resources where the principal must be preserved and earnings benefit the government or its citizens. Choice A is incorrect because internal service funds are classified as proprietary funds, not governmental funds.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Economic resources measurement focus
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, the economic resources measurement focus is used by which category of funds?', '["Only governmental funds", "Proprietary funds and fiduciary funds", "Only fiduciary funds", "All fund types including governmental funds"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, proprietary funds and fiduciary funds use the economic resources measurement focus, which recognizes all assets and liabilities including long-term items and reports revenues and expenses on the accrual basis. Governmental funds use the current financial resources measurement focus instead. Choice D is incorrect because governmental funds use the current financial resources measurement focus, which recognizes only current assets and liabilities and reports revenues and expenditures rather than expenses.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Budgetary comparison variance
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Westdale adopts an annual General Fund budget with estimated revenues of $32,000,000 and appropriations of $30,500,000. Actual revenues total $33,200,000 and actual expenditures total $31,100,000. Under GASB 34, what variance should the city report for expenditures in the budgetary comparison schedule?', '["$600,000 favorable, because actual expenditures were less than estimated revenues", "$900,000 favorable, because actual expenditures were $900,000 below estimated revenues", "$1,100,000 favorable, because actual expenditures were below actual revenues", "$600,000 unfavorable, because actual expenditures exceeded appropriations"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, paragraph 130, the budgetary comparison schedule compares actual inflows and outflows to the legally adopted budget. For expenditures, the variance is actual ($31,100,000) minus appropriations ($30,500,000) = $600,000 unfavorable because spending exceeded the authorized amount. Choice A is incorrect because expenditure variances are measured against appropriations, not estimated revenues — spending $600,000 more than appropriated is unfavorable regardless of the revenue outcome.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — GASB 33 reimbursement-basis grant revenue
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'On March 1, 2026, the Riverside Foundation awards a $1,500,000 reimbursement-basis grant to the City of Riverside for youth center construction. The grant requires the city to incur and document qualifying construction expenditures before requesting reimbursement. As of the June 30, 2026, fiscal year-end, the city has incurred $420,000 in qualifying construction costs and received $420,000 in reimbursement. Under GASB 33, how much grant revenue should the city recognize in the General Fund for fiscal year 2026?', '["$420,000, because reimbursement-type grants are recognized as revenue when qualifying expenditures are incurred and eligibility requirements are met", "$1,500,000, because the full grant award creates a receivable when the agreement is signed", "$750,000, because 50% of the grant period has elapsed", "$0, because no revenue is recognized until the project is complete and final costs are determined"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 33, paragraph 20, voluntary nonexchange transactions with reimbursement eligibility requirements (allowable cost provisions) are recognized as revenue when the qualifying expenditures are incurred because that is when the eligibility requirement is met. The city incurred $420,000 in qualifying costs, making that amount eligible for recognition. Choice B is incorrect because reimbursement-type grants do not create receivables at the award date — revenue recognition depends on incurring allowable costs, not on the existence of the grant agreement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Deferred inflows/outflows effect on net position
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Pine Valley reports the following pension-related items under GASB 68: net pension liability of $14,200,000, deferred outflows of resources related to pensions of $3,800,000, and deferred inflows of resources related to pensions of $2,100,000. The finance director asks how the deferred items affect the city''s total net position compared to reporting only the net pension liability. What is the net effect of the deferred items on net position?', '["Net position decreases by $1,700,000 because deferred outflows represent future expenses", "Net position increases by $5,900,000 because both deferred items reduce the pension obligation", "Net position is unaffected because deferred outflows and deferred inflows offset the net pension liability", "Net position increases by $1,700,000 because deferred outflows exceed deferred inflows"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 63, deferred outflows of resources increase net position while deferred inflows of resources decrease net position. Net position equals assets plus deferred outflows minus liabilities minus deferred inflows. Compared to recognizing only the net pension liability, the deferred outflows ($3,800,000) increase net position and the deferred inflows ($2,100,000) decrease it, for a net improvement of $1,700,000. Choice A is incorrect because while deferred outflows will eventually become expenses, they currently function as asset-like items that increase net position.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
